sections.body:
Java evaluates an if/else if/else chain strictly in order, from top to bottom, and executes the block belonging to the **first** condition that evaluates to true -- every subsequent else if and else in that same chain is then skipped entirely, even if a later condition would also have been true.

```java
int score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");   // this one runs
} else if (score >= 70) {
    System.out.println("C");   // never even checked, because a branch above already matched
} else {
    System.out.println("F");
}
```

Order matters enormously here: if the `score >= 70` check were written *above* `score >= 80`, a score of 85 would incorrectly print "C", since that broader condition is also technically true and it would be reached first. A related, easy-to-miss hazard is the so-called dangling else: when an if has no braces and is nested inside another if with no braces, it can become genuinely ambiguous to a human reader (though not to the compiler, which always attaches an else to the nearest unmatched if) which if an else actually belongs to. The reliable habit that sidesteps this entirely is to always use braces `{ }` around if/else bodies, even for a single statement -- it costs nothing and removes an entire category of misreading.

A classic `switch` compares one value against a series of `case` labels and jumps to the matching one -- but execution does not stop there automatically. Without an explicit `break`, control **falls through** into the next case's code, and the one after that, and so on, until it hits a break or runs out of cases entirely. This was an original, deliberate design choice (it lets several case labels intentionally share one block of code by stacking labels with no code between them), but in ordinary use it is one of the most common sources of Java bugs, precisely because it's easy to forget a `break` and get silently, extra behavior with no error of any kind.

```java
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");
    case 4:
        System.out.println("Thursday");
    default:
        System.out.println("Some other day");
}
```

With `day == 3`, this prints "Wednesday", then falls straight through into "Thursday" and "Some other day" too, because none of those three cases has a `break` stopping it. `default` is not required syntactically, but leaving it out means a value matching no case simply does nothing at all -- silently -- which is almost always worth guarding against explicitly rather than leaving as an implicit no-op.

Java 14 introduced a genuinely different form of `switch` that is an **expression** -- it directly produces a value you can assign or return -- using `->` instead of `:` and `break`. Each case in this form is an independent branch with no fall-through possible at all; there's no missing-break bug to even make, because there's nothing to fall through into.

```java
int day = 3;
String name = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    case 3 -> "Wednesday";
    default -> "Some other day";
};
System.out.println(name);   // Wednesday, and only Wednesday
```

For a case that needs more than one expression's worth of logic, a braced block after `->` can compute a value and hand it back with `yield`: `case 3 -> { System.out.println("matched three"); yield "Wednesday"; }`. Beyond just being safer, using switch as an *expression* (assigning its result directly to a variable, as above) is often more readable than the classic statement form, because it makes clear, structurally, that every branch is contributing to one single value rather than performing scattered side effects. The classic colon-and-break form still exists and still works exactly as it always has -- for statement-style, multi-branch side effects, or in codebases targeting older Java versions -- but the arrow form is generally the better default for new code once you can use it.

