sections.body:
A classic for loop's header has three parts, and it's worth being precise about the order they actually run in, because it's not simply top-to-bottom repeated: `for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { ... }` runs `int i = 0` exactly **once**, at the very start; then it repeatedly checks `i < 3`, and if true, runs the body, then runs `i++`, then goes back and checks `i < 3` again -- and stops the moment that check is false, without running the body one more time.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    System.out.println("i = " + i);
}
// i is out of scope here -- it only exists within the for statement itself
```

This prints `i = 0`, `i = 1`, `i = 2` -- three lines, not four, because the moment `i` becomes 3, the condition check happens *before* the body would run again, and fails, ending the loop. Writing `i <= 3` instead would run one extra iteration (`i = 3` included) -- this exact kind of boundary slip is where off-by-one bugs come from, and reading the condition literally, rather than assuming what it 'probably means,' is the reliable way to avoid them. The loop variable declared in the header (`i`, here) is scoped to the for statement alone -- it doesn't exist before it and doesn't exist after it, which is deliberate: it prevents accidental reuse of a leftover loop counter later in the method.

`while` checks its condition **before** every single iteration, including the very first one -- if the condition is already false the first time it's checked, the body never runs at all, not even once. `do-while` checks its condition **after** running the body -- guaranteeing the body executes at least once, no matter what, before the condition is ever consulted.

```java
int attempts = 0;
while (attempts >= 5) {
    System.out.println("this never prints -- condition is false immediately");
}

int tries = 0;
do {
    tries++;
    System.out.println("attempt number " + tries);
} while (tries < 3);
// prints: attempt number 1, attempt number 2, attempt number 3
```

The practical case where this distinction actually matters is when the very thing you want to check depends on something that only exists *after* the body has run once -- a classic example being 'prompt the user for input, then keep re-prompting until it's valid': there's no meaningful input to validate before the first prompt has actually happened, which is precisely the shape do-while is built for. Using a plain while for that same pattern usually forces you to duplicate the prompt-and-read logic once before the loop, just to have something to check going into the first condition test -- do-while avoids that duplication by construction.

`break` exits the nearest enclosing loop (or switch) immediately and completely -- execution jumps straight to the first statement after that loop, skipping any remaining iterations entirely. `continue` is weaker and easier to misjudge: it does **not** exit the loop -- it only skips the *rest of the current iteration's body*, then jumps straight to the next iteration's condition check (running the update step first, in a for loop). The loop itself keeps going.

Both normally target the loop they're textually inside of, but nested loops sometimes need to affect an *outer* loop from inside an inner one -- that's what a **label** is for: a name placed before a loop that `break` or `continue` can reference directly.

```java
int target = 5;
boolean found = false;

search:
for (int row = 0; row < 3; row++) {
    for (int col = 0; col < 3; col++) {
        int value = row * 3 + col;
        if (value == target) {
            System.out.println("found at row " + row + ", col " + col);
            found = true;
            break search;   // exits BOTH loops at once
        }
    }
}
```

An unlabeled `break` inside the inner loop here would only exit the inner `for`, and the outer loop would simply continue on to its next row, needlessly checking columns for rows that no longer matter once the target's already been found. `break search;` exits both loops in a single step, which is exactly the intended behavior and avoids needing an extra `if (found) break;` check duplicated in the outer loop too.

For simply visiting every element of an array (or any `Iterable`) in order, the enhanced for-each loop is shorter and harder to get wrong: `for (int value : numbers) { ... }` reads as 'for each value in numbers,' with no index variable, no condition, and no update step to manage yourself.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};
int sum = 0;
for (int value : numbers) {
    sum += value;
}
System.out.println(sum);   // 60
```

The cost is real, though: you lose direct access to the current index (no way to know 'this is element 2' just from `value` alone, without maintaining a separate counter yourself), and for an array of primitives, `value` inside the loop is a **copy** of each element for that iteration -- reassigning `value` has zero effect on the underlying array, since `value` is not a reference back into it. If you need the index (to print positions, to compare adjacent elements, to skip every other one) or you need to actually mutate elements in place, you need the classic indexed `for` loop, writing through `numbers[i] = ...` directly, rather than the for-each form.

An infinite loop is not some special, separate kind of loop -- it's simply an ordinary loop whose condition never becomes false, usually because of one specific, recurring mistake: forgetting the update step entirely, updating the wrong variable (one the condition doesn't actually depend on), or writing an update that moves the wrong direction relative to the condition.

```java
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    // forgot: i++;   -- i never changes, condition never becomes false
}
```

A for loop's header groups all three parts together specifically so this is harder to forget, but it's still entirely possible -- `for (int i = 0; i < 5; i--)` compiles perfectly fine and also runs forever, because decrementing `i` moves it further away from ever failing `i < 5`, not closer. When a loop hangs and never produces expected output, the update step relative to the condition is the very first thing worth checking: does this loop's own logic guarantee the condition eventually flips to false, or is that just an assumption?

